[{"data":1,"prerenderedAt":1019},["ShallowReactive",2],{"/blog/tabs-garden-tab-suggestions":3},{"id":4,"title":5,"body":6,"canonical_url":997,"categories":998,"cover_image":997,"date":1001,"description":1002,"draft":1003,"extension":1004,"external_site":997,"external_url":997,"img":997,"meta":1005,"navigation":327,"path":1012,"seo":1013,"stem":1014,"tags":1015,"__hash__":1018},"content/blog/tabs-garden-tab-suggestions.md","Adding tab suggestions using cosine-similarity on tabs.garden",{"type":7,"value":8,"toc":988},"minimark",[9,18,23,26,37,40,44,47,50,53,177,180,183,187,190,201,204,210,213,526,530,533,599,602,689,692,695,914,918,921,924,930,937,941,948,962,965,969,972,975,978,981,984],[10,11,12,13,17],"p",{},"A new feature on [tabs.garden]",[14,15,16],"span",{},"tabs.garden"," just landed, where a user can see similar tab suggestions in the sidebar. This was implemented in a very simple way (spoiler: using JSON files and a rudimentary cosine-similarity score), but I was very pleased by the result.",[19,20,22],"h2",{"id":21},"the-data-at-our-disposal","The data at our disposal",[10,24,25],{},"The guitar tab data has a few things - the title, artist, when it was released, and then the tabs themselves.",[27,28,34],"pre",{"className":29,"code":31,"language":32,"meta":33},[30],"language-text","title: Bless The Telephone\nartist: Labi Siffre\nrelease: 1971\nkey: G\ncapo: 5\nchords: G D Em C ...\n","text","",[35,36,31],"code",{"__ignoreMap":33},[10,38,39],{},"While this information is easy to parse as a human, to compute a similarity with other tabs we need to first encode this into something numerical; that is often called an \"embedding\".",[19,41,43],{"id":42},"what-is-an-embedding","What is an embedding?",[10,45,46],{},"An embedding is a numeric representation of something.",[10,48,49],{},"In machine learning, people often talk about text embeddings, where a sentence gets turned into a list of numbers. In this case we did something similar where each tab is turned into a sparse vector.",[10,51,52],{},"A sparse vector is just a map of feature names to numeric weights. For example, a song might become something like:",[27,54,58],{"className":55,"code":56,"language":57,"meta":33,"style":33},"language-json shiki shiki-themes github-dark","{\n  \"genre:worship\": 3.5,\n  \"chord:G:maj\": 0.4,\n  \"chord:D:maj\": 0.4,\n  \"chord:E:min\": 0.4,\n  \"progression:G:maj>D:maj\": 0.6,\n  \"release-decade:2010s\": 2.4,\n  \"release-window:2010-2014\": 1.8,\n  \"key:g\": 0.8\n}\n","json",[35,59,60,68,84,97,109,121,134,147,160,171],{"__ignoreMap":33},[14,61,64],{"class":62,"line":63},"line",1,[14,65,67],{"class":66},"s95oV","{\n",[14,69,71,75,78,81],{"class":62,"line":70},2,[14,72,74],{"class":73},"sDLfK","  \"genre:worship\"",[14,76,77],{"class":66},": ",[14,79,80],{"class":73},"3.5",[14,82,83],{"class":66},",\n",[14,85,87,90,92,95],{"class":62,"line":86},3,[14,88,89],{"class":73},"  \"chord:G:maj\"",[14,91,77],{"class":66},[14,93,94],{"class":73},"0.4",[14,96,83],{"class":66},[14,98,100,103,105,107],{"class":62,"line":99},4,[14,101,102],{"class":73},"  \"chord:D:maj\"",[14,104,77],{"class":66},[14,106,94],{"class":73},[14,108,83],{"class":66},[14,110,112,115,117,119],{"class":62,"line":111},5,[14,113,114],{"class":73},"  \"chord:E:min\"",[14,116,77],{"class":66},[14,118,94],{"class":73},[14,120,83],{"class":66},[14,122,124,127,129,132],{"class":62,"line":123},6,[14,125,126],{"class":73},"  \"progression:G:maj>D:maj\"",[14,128,77],{"class":66},[14,130,131],{"class":73},"0.6",[14,133,83],{"class":66},[14,135,137,140,142,145],{"class":62,"line":136},7,[14,138,139],{"class":73},"  \"release-decade:2010s\"",[14,141,77],{"class":66},[14,143,144],{"class":73},"2.4",[14,146,83],{"class":66},[14,148,150,153,155,158],{"class":62,"line":149},8,[14,151,152],{"class":73},"  \"release-window:2010-2014\"",[14,154,77],{"class":66},[14,156,157],{"class":73},"1.8",[14,159,83],{"class":66},[14,161,163,166,168],{"class":62,"line":162},9,[14,164,165],{"class":73},"  \"key:g\"",[14,167,77],{"class":66},[14,169,170],{"class":73},"0.8\n",[14,172,174],{"class":62,"line":173},10,[14,175,176],{"class":66},"}\n",[10,178,179],{},"It is sparse because the total set of possible features is large, but any given song only has a small subset of them.",[10,181,182],{},"The benefit of using something like this, is that we can still easily inspect the data. If two songs are similar because of the genre, I can see that, and it also makes it easy to adjust the weights for any one property.",[19,184,186],{"id":185},"cosine-similarity","Cosine similarity",[10,188,189],{},"Once each tab is represented as a vector, we then need a way to compare them.",[10,191,192,193,196,197,200],{},"A very popular way to do that is using something called \"cosine similarity\". It measures whether two vectors point in the same direction. A score close to ",[35,194,195],{},"1"," means the vectors have a lot in common. A score close to ",[35,198,199],{},"0"," means they do not.",[10,202,203],{},"The formula looks something like this:",[27,205,208],{"className":206,"code":207,"language":32,"meta":33},[30],"cosine_similarity(A, B) = dot(A, B) / (magnitude(A) * magnitude(B))\n",[35,209,207],{"__ignoreMap":33},[10,211,212],{},"In the code, it's implementation with this small function:",[27,214,218],{"className":215,"code":216,"language":217,"meta":33,"style":33},"language-ts shiki shiki-themes github-dark","export const cosineSimilarity = (\n  left: SparseVector,\n  right: SparseVector,\n): number => {\n  let dot = 0;\n  let leftMagnitude = 0;\n  let rightMagnitude = 0;\n\n  Object.entries(left).forEach(([key, value]) => {\n    dot += value * (right[key] ?? 0);\n    leftMagnitude += value * value;\n  });\n  Object.values(right).forEach((value) => {\n    rightMagnitude += value * value;\n  });\n\n  if (leftMagnitude === 0 || rightMagnitude === 0) return 0;\n  return dot / (Math.sqrt(leftMagnitude) * Math.sqrt(rightMagnitude));\n};\n","ts",[35,219,220,239,253,264,280,2